  "transcript": "User: Hey! So I'm Sofia — I run a real estate office here in Houston. Your ad was talking about missed calls? That's literally me.\nAgent: Got it, Sofia — missed calls are literally you, I hear that a lot from busy brokerages. When a buyer or seller calls and you can’t grab it in the moment, what usually happens to that call — voicemail, missed altogether, someone else picking it up?\nUser: Oh, voicemail usually, but sometimes I just miss them completely. I mean, maybe 25 or 30 a week?\nAgent: Mhm, makes sense — 25–30 a week going to voicemail or missed completely adds up fast. When those callers don’t get you live, do they usually end up calling back… or do you feel like they just move on to another agent or office?\nUser: Honestly, I'm not sure. Some call back, but I bet a lot just move on. It's frustrating!\nAgent: Yeah, I hear you — not knowing which ones slipped away is the frustrating part. Has anyone ever walked you through what those missed calls are actually costing you in dollars, or has it mostly just been a gut feeling so far?\nUser: Mostly a gut feeling. No one's broken it down in dollars. I'm curious — tell me more about that!\nAgent: Got it, so it’s been more of a “I know this hurts” feeling than real numbers. So ballpark for your office — of those 25–30 calls a week that hit voicemail or get missed, what percentage do you think would actually turn into a legit client or appointment if you answered live… maybe half, around 60%, what feels right?\nUser: Yeah, maybe around 50% could turn into clients. Honestly, a live answer could make a big difference.\nAgent: Got it, 50% makes sense — a live answer is huge in real estate. And when you do land a new client from one of those calls, what’s that worth to you on average — are we talking more like $3,000, $5,000, $10,000+ in commission over the relationship?\nUser: Easily $5,000 to $10,000+ per client. That's why missing calls is a real pain for me!\nAgent: So with your own numbers, that’s 